<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    ================================================================================================
    PowerFramework.DataServices.Tests

    Unit and service level test project for the DataServices service of the PowerFramework .NET 10
    decomposition. It characterizes the behaviour ported from the legacy PowerBuilder library
    `pfw.datawindow.services.pbl` (13 objects, zero native bindings, pure PowerScript):

      * the 22 event DataWindow chain: 9 semantic events plus 13 raw `pbm_dwn*` events
      * the item change micro protocol: the {0, 1, 2, 3} alphabet reproduced verbatim as FOUR
        DISTINCT ARMS, because PowerScript `choose case` does not fall through - case 1 is an
        EMPTY arm that returns 1 unchanged and restores nothing, case 2 restores value and status
        only when the value was unchanged, case 3 keeps the value and rewrites the result to 1, and
        the default arm coerces by column type prefix, fires the changed event and forcibly returns
        2 [se_cst_dw.sru:L211-L252]. A test that expected case 1 to restore would be asserting a
        fall through the language does not have
      * static single sigil versus dynamic double sigil column expression expansion, which is the
        one distinction that does not survive naive serialization
      * the five column type validators: date, datetime, number, string and time
      * the headless halves of RowSelect, ColumnSort, ContextMenu and DropDownSearch

    Behavioural oracle for these tests. The legacy tree is READ ONLY and is never an edit target:
      * ws_objects/pfw.tests.pbl.src/w_test_dwsvc_columnexp.srw
          L151 static expansion, L154 dynamic expansion, L157 dynamic indirect expansion together
          with a foreign variable, L143 the cross DataWindow `of_AddForeignVar` live pointer
          fixture, L292 set variable and recalculate, L305 the macro invocation callback,
          L315 the expression trace carrying the call stack.
      * ws_objects/pfw.tests.pbl.src/w_test_dwsvc_rowselect.srw
          L42 the composable row select style bitmask.

    HAND AUTHORED ON PURPOSE. This file is not templated and must not be regenerated from a
    template. the pinned SDK ships no `xunit3` template, and `dotnet new xunit` scaffolds the xunit v2
    line with an older test SDK and an older coverage collector. Both were confirmed on this
    toolchain, so the xunit.v3 project shape is written out explicitly here.

    EVERYTHING GENERIC IS INHERITED, NOTHING IS RESTATED. The target runtime (net10.0), the C#
    language level, the nullable reference context, the implicit global usings, the warnings as
    errors gate, the .NET analyzers and central package management all arrive from the repository
    root `Directory.Build.props` by directory walk up, and every package version arrives from the
    repository root `Directory.Packages.props`. Re-declaring any of them here would create a second
    source of truth that can silently drift, so none of them appears below. Warnings are errors here
    too: a test project is held to exactly the same bar as the application it tests.

    THE MANDATED PER SERVICE COMMAND MUST KEEP WORKING VERBATIM, with no extra arguments, run from
    the `services/dataservices-service` directory: `dotnet restore`, then `dotnet build -c Release`,
    then `dotnet test` collecting "XPlat Code Coverage".

    Those resolve without an explicit project argument only because `dataservices-service.slnx` is
    the single solution file in the service directory and enumerates exactly this project plus
    `PowerFramework.DataServices`. No solution filter is used or referenced anywhere in this
    service, because a filter's project list has to be kept in exact agreement with the solution BY
    HAND and every drift fails hard. This note used to say the legacy filter format fails with
    MSB4014 against the XML solution format in all three verbs; that was measured false on the
    pinned SDK - see docs/BUILD.md section 2.
    ================================================================================================
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      MANDATORY. xunit.v3 test projects must be executable, and this line is the authoritative
      declaration of that requirement: the xunit.v3 build assets carry a `_XunitValidateBuild`
      target that raises a hard MSBuild error when `OutputType` is not `Exe`, and both the auto
      generated entry point and the app host depend on it.

      Keep this line even though it can look redundant in a trace. Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 18.8.1
      also assigns `Exe` from its own targets file, which is imported after this project body and
      therefore wins, so on today's exact package set the xunit guard never actually fires. That
      override is an undocumented implementation detail gated on unrelated WinUI and UWP switches,
      so leaning on it would leave this project one package bump away from tripping the guard.
      Design time tooling and any evaluation performed before restore also read the value declared
      here rather than the overridden one. Never relax it to `Library`.
    -->
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>

    <!-- A test assembly is never shipped as a NuGet package. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

    <!--
      Deliberately NOT set here, because each is already supplied elsewhere and a duplicate would
      be a second source of truth:
        * The root namespace and assembly name both default to this project's file name,
          PowerFramework.DataServices.Tests, which is exactly what is wanted.
        * The test project marker is inferred by Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk.
        * The app host switch and the generated program file switch are both forced by the xunit.v3
          build assets, which also generate the entry point, so this project intentionally contains
          no Program.cs of its own.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The complete test stack: exactly five packages, every one of them VERSIONLESS. Central Package
    Management is enabled repository wide, so the versions live only in Directory.Packages.props
    (xunit.v3 3.2.2, xunit.runner.visualstudio 3.1.5, Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 18.8.1,
    coverlet.collector 10.0.1, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing 10.0.11). Pinning a version here
    instead would raise NU1008 and fail the build.

    No sixth package. There is deliberately no mocking library and no fluent assertion library
    anywhere in the dependency inventory: the sibling test files use hand written test doubles and
    plain xunit `Assert`, which keeps parity assertions explicit and auditable against the legacy
    oracle. A sixth package would also fail restore outright, because Directory.Packages.props
    carries no `PackageVersion` entry for it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Test stack">
    <!-- Test framework: the xunit v3 line, supplying the in process runner and the assertions. -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />

    <!-- Test adapter, so `dotnet test` discovers xunit.v3 tests. Development time asset only. -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>

    <!-- Test host and the `dotnet test` integration. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />

    <!--
      The coverage collector. This reference is what makes `dotnet test` with the "XPlat Code
      Coverage" collector emit `coverage.cobertura.xml` under TestResults, which is the exact
      artifact the 80 percent line coverage per service gate is measured from in CI. Without it the
      gate cannot be evaluated at all, so this entry is a requirement rather than a convenience.
      Its collector registration ships in the package's `build` assets, which is why `build` is
      retained in IncludeAssets; `PrivateAssets` only stops the asset flowing onward to consumers of
      this project and therefore does not affect collection here.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>

    <!--
      In process host for the service level tests that sit alongside the unit tests, giving
      WebApplicationFactory over the real DataServices pipeline so the gRPC surface, the thin REST
      projection consumed by Gateway, the anonymous /health endpoint and the JWT protected
      /v1/ping endpoint are all exercised without a container or a network listener.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Exactly one project reference, and it is the service under test. Everything else the tests need
    arrives transitively through it: PowerFramework.Contracts and the shared Kernel, Diagnostics,
    Eventful, Localization and Containers libraries. Referencing any of them directly would
    duplicate the dependency graph, and referencing another service would break the rule that the
    published contracts project is the only coupling permitted across a service boundary.

    Deliberately absent, and each absence is a decision rather than an oversight:
      * No protocol definition item and no gRPC package. PowerFramework.Contracts compiles
        Proto/dataservices.v1.proto, Proto/persistence.v1.proto and Proto/common.v1.proto once,
        generating both the server bases and the client stubs. Re-declaring the protocol definitions
        here would generate the same types a second time and break the build.
      * No internals visibility attribute declared here. The grant only ever runs the other way:
        the application project grants internals visibility to this assembly so the tests can reach
        its non public types, and this project never grants anything to anyone. WebApplicationFactory
        binds to the application's generated entry point type, which the single project reference
        below already makes visible; note also that the xunit.v3 auto generated entry point is
        emitted as `AutoGenerated.XunitAutoGeneratedEntryPoint` inside this assembly's own
        namespace, so it cannot collide with the application's entry point type name.
      * No Entity Framework Core reference, no ADO level SQLite provider and no native SQLite
        bundle. Persistence is the only service in the system that holds a storage provider;
        DataServices reaches it over gRPC, and these tests provision no database at all.
      * No token minting library. Security is the sole token issuer in the system. DataServices
        holds verification material only, and the sibling tests authenticate through a test
        authentication scheme rather than by minting a token.
      * No secret, key, password, connection string or certificate of any kind. Nothing in this
        project is security sensitive, and all real key material is injected through the options
        pattern from the orchestration secret layer at run time.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Service under test">
    <ProjectReference Include="../PowerFramework.DataServices/PowerFramework.DataServices.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>


  <!--
    REPOSITORY ROOT, EMBEDDED SO THIS PROJECT'S ON DISK LOCATORS DO NOT DEPEND ON WHERE ITS OUTPUT WENT
    This project reads real files from the source tree, and it finds them by walking up from
    AppContext.BaseDirectory for a marker. That walk holds only while the output sits inside the
    checkout, so a test run whose artifacts path option names a directory outside the tree - a supported
    and entirely valid way to run the suite - left every one of those locators with no ancestor
    carrying the marker, and they threw for a reason unrelated to the code under test.

    The value comes from the PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot property in the repository root
    Directory.Build.props, which resolves it from that file's own location. It is turned into assembly
    metadata HERE, in each test project, rather than there: a service assembly ships inside a container
    image and must carry no build machine path, and the root property sheet keeps its documented rule of
    introducing no project level item. The locators still verify their marker and still keep the walk as
    the fallback, so an absent or stale value degrades to the previous behaviour rather than misreading
    a different tree.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot" Value="$(PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot)" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
